# Compute

Short work stays on CPU: unit tests, function-preserving checks, and toy notebooks that finish in well under a minute. Do not put those on the GPU. Those notebooks run on the Mac.

Heavy training is a PC step. Say so before handing it over: switch to the CUDA machine, set `device: cuda`, and stay inside the 0.20 memory cap. Do not start that run on the Mac and call it the canonical result. The Mac can repeat a short smoke on MPS; label it MPS and do not pool it with CUDA.

Default memory fraction is 0.20 via `apply_compute_cap`. Do not call `torch.cuda.set_per_process_memory_fraction` anywhere else. Fractions above 0.25 require `allow_high_memory` in the config. The CUDA machine may be shared. The cap stays in place during heavy training.

Do not launch a heavy run while changing code. Run the CPU smoke first. Mark a real CUDA test with `pytest.mark.gpu`. The default pytest command excludes that marker.

Runs are resumable. If a run would exceed the cap, record that and stop. Do not raise the cap to finish a curve. Never hard-code `cuda`. `auto` is for smoke. An explicit `cuda` request fails on the Mac instead of silently switching devices.
